WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.320
We have a very interesting new poll out&nbsp;
of Quinnipiac University about whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.320
Republicans want Donald Trump to remain&nbsp;
involved in American politics and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.840
Republican Party politics for the foreseeable&nbsp;
future. And the answer is yes, they do.&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.960
Yes, they do. Three quarters of Republicans want&nbsp;
Donald Trump to continue playing a prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.080
role in the Republican Party. The poll showed&nbsp;
89 percent of Republicans opposed the conviction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:39.360
of Donald Trump in his Senate second Senate&nbsp;
impeachment trial. 87 percent of Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.640
say Trump should be allowed to hold public&nbsp;
office in the future. Eighty nine percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.240
Republicans believe Trump was not responsible for&nbsp;
inciting the violence that led to the January 6th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:59.120
riots. And among all Americans polled, 55&nbsp;
percent oppose Trump. Republican returning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:08.400
public office. So this is a very important number&nbsp;
because we talked last week or the week before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:17.120
about 64 percent of Republicans saying if&nbsp;
Trump left the party and started his own party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.680
we would follow him. Nearly two thirds of the&nbsp;
Republican Party has such soft support for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:30.160
party or has simply accepted Donald Trump as the&nbsp;
sort of lord and savior deity head of the party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.240
at this point that they would follow him out of&nbsp;
it. That's not so good for the Republican Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.240
as an institution, but it's certainly good if&nbsp;
you're Donald Trump and you want to control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.840
the Republican Party or American politics going&nbsp;
forward. Now, we learn that although there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.680
some Republicans who would not leave&nbsp;
the Republican Party to follow Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.600
many of them do want Trump to continue&nbsp;
involved in American politics. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.600
one important caveat is that we know from our&nbsp;
conversation with David Shorr a few weeks ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.840
that Republicans who answer&nbsp;
the phone and talk to pollsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.240
are not necessarily representative of all&nbsp;
Republicans. And David Shorr believes that some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.880
of the more Trump and Kuhnen conspiracy types may&nbsp;
be refusing to participate in polls. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.400
pollsters try to and believe they have ways of&nbsp;
accounting and adjusting for that. But David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.680
Shaw was skeptical that they really are great at&nbsp;
it. So that's something, something to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:38.560
But assuming these numbers are roughly accurate,&nbsp;
this is really bad for Republicans as a party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:46.000
They lost the presidential election by almost&nbsp;
eight million people. And at this point in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.320
most of them want Trump to stay involved and two&nbsp;
thirds are even willing to leave the party. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.720
don't know how the math works out for them. And&nbsp;
what I mean by that is if you lose a national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.720
election by eight million, in great part because&nbsp;
of the guy who's at the head of the party, Donald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.040
Trump, failing on coronavirus, turning off so many&nbsp;
traditional Republicans, you lose by eight million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.320
and then you want that guy to stay involved. And&nbsp;
if he leaves the party, you'll leave with him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.360
Those are not numbers that bode well for the&nbsp;
Republican Party in national elections. Now, at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:28.000
the local level, it's a very different situation.&nbsp;
You can still, you know, the Trump factor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.160
extremely red areas that would never consider&nbsp;
voting for, for example, a Democratic senator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:41.120
It may in practice not have that big of an impact.&nbsp;
But for as long as Donald Trump is so prominent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:48.720
it looks really tough for the Republican Party to&nbsp;
win national elections. And I'm glad to have my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.920
mind changed, but I don't see how the numbers&nbsp;
work out now as a matter of sort of following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:00.640
the nature or the easy nature by which so many&nbsp;
Republican voters were bamboozled by Donald Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.640
They fell for the populist sounding rhetoric&nbsp;
back in 2015 and 2016. We've talked about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.400
many times. Yeah, they fell for it. And then Trump&nbsp;
didn't do it. But they still don't seem to realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:21.520
that Trump has done nothing for just your average&nbsp;
working class Republican beyond. Yes. Making them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:27.840
really afraid of others that are going to do bad&nbsp;
things to you. China, undocumented immigrants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.840
Democrats, communists, Marxists, anarchists or&nbsp;
anarchists, as Donald Trump would call them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.680
yeah, he stoked fears for the average&nbsp;
working class Republican really well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.680
he told them lies about the things&nbsp;
he would do, said he would fix trade.&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.040
He didn't do it, said he would build a wall across&nbsp;
the entire U.S. Mexico border. He didn't do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.640
said he would replace Obamacare with a&nbsp;
beautiful new health care plan, didn't do it.&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:58.880
So fear, false promises, a coalescing the&nbsp;
most hateful elements of the Republican Party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.040
But they still seem not to realize that&nbsp;
he did nothing for them economically the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.360
way he claimed to do. And now, as we know, 40&nbsp;
percent of coronavirus deaths during the Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:18.480
administration were unnecessary and avoidable.&nbsp;
So, of course, all of the numbers. Wow, how does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.840
the Republican Party win national elections in&nbsp;
these circumstances? But the other question is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.440
millions, tens of millions still don't realize&nbsp;
Trump didn't do any of the things he said he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.160
do for the working class. Do they care? Do they&nbsp;
not know? I'm not sure. I don't know the answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.360
but if they never wake up to it, support for Trump&nbsp;
in the Republican Party is likely to continue

